"""/guides/ pages."""

LAND_STEPS = """<div class="prose">
<p><strong>1. Get the facts straight before you post.</strong> Confirm the exact size in square metres, the title document (C of O, Governor's Consent, registered survey, gazette or excision), whether the land is free of government acquisition, and who has authority to sell. Ask the seller for copies you can show a buyer's lawyer.</p>
<p><strong>2. Shoot proof, not just bush.</strong> Walk the access road on video, film beacons if they're in place, take a drone or wide shot if you can, and photograph nearby landmarks so buyers can place it. Blur personal details on any document you show.</p>
<p><strong>3. Write one master ad, then adapt it.</strong> Location, size, title, access, price per plot, payment plan, inspection day, contact. Every channel version below is a cut of that same fact sheet.</p>
<p><strong>4. Post where Nigerian land buyers already look.</strong> The Nairaland Properties board, Jiji, Nigeria Property Centre and PropertyPro listings, Facebook property groups, your WhatsApp status and broadcast lists, Instagram and TikTok. Realtor networks run by developers are the biggest multiplier if you're marketing an estate.</p>
<p><strong>5. Run fixed site inspections.</strong> A free group inspection every Saturday with a pick-up point converts far better than \"come anytime\". Collect WhatsApp numbers from everyone who registers.</p>
<p><strong>6. Make verification easy.</strong> Invite buyers to verify documents with their own lawyer, and only accept payment into a company or clearly identified account. Transparency is your best sales tool in a market full of land scams.</p>
<p><strong>7. Follow up within 24 hours.</strong> Send every enquirer and inspection attendee the layout, price, payment plan and a deadline if one genuinely exists.</p>
</div>"""

